        "content": "     1→[\n     2→  {\n     3→    \"title\": \"Niteshift Raises $7M Seed to Build AI Coding Tools Without Big AI Lock-In\",\n     4→    \"description\": \"Datadog veterans launched AI coding startup Niteshift with a $7 million seed round led by Greylock's Jerry Chen. The startup is betting against big AI lock-in, positioning itself as an alternative in the competitive AI coding agent market alongside players like Cursor, Windsurf, and the major tech companies.\",\n     5→    \"author\": \"TechCrunch\",\n     6→    \"source\": \"TechMedia\",\n     7→    \"url\": \"https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/10/datadog-veterans-launch-ai-coding-startup-niteshift-on-a-bet-against-big-ai-lock-in/\",\n     8→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-10\",\n     9→    \"topics\": [\"ai-coding\", \"startups\", \"developer-tools\", \"venture-capital\"],\n    10→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    11→  },\n    12→  {\n    13→    \"title\": \"Best AI Coding Agent (2026): Ranked by Terminal-Bench, Price, and Performance\",\n    14→    \"description\": \"Terminal-Bench (tbench.ai) published comprehensive rankings of AI coding tools showing Codex CLI with GPT-5.5 leading at 83.4%, followed by Claude Code with Opus 4.8 at 78.9%. The benchmark evaluates multiple AI coding agents across terminal-based coding tasks.\",\n    15→    \"author\": \"MorphLLM\",\n    16→    \"source\": \"Technical Benchmark\",\n    17→    \"url\": \"https://www.morphllm.com/ai-coding-agent\",\n    18→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-09\",\n    19→    \"topics\": [\"ai-coding\", \"benchmarking\", \"performance-metrics\", \"llms\"],\n    20→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    21→  },\n    22→  {\n    23→    \"title\": \"Claude Code Security Best Practices for Enterprise Teams\",\n    24→    \"description\": \"Updated security guidelines for enterprise deployment of Claude Code, covering SSO integration, AI governance, and best practices for securing AI coding workflows. The guide addresses enterprise concerns around data protection, access controls, and monitoring.\",\n    25→    \"author\": \"TrueFoundry\",\n    26→    \"source\": \"Technical Blog\",\n    27→    \"url\": \"https://www.truefoundry.com/blog/claude-code-security-best-practices\",\n    28→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-09\",\n    29→    \"topics\": [\"ai-security\", \"enterprise-deployment\", \"governance\", \"claude-code\"],\n    30→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    31→  },\n    32→  {\n    33→    \"title\": \"Top 10 AI Coding CLI Tools in 2026 - A Complete Developer's Guide\",\n    34→    \"description\": \"Comprehensive guide covering the current AI coding CLI landscape with a decision framework for developers. Includes analysis of terminal-based AI coding assistants, comparison of features, and guidance on tool selection.\",\n    35→    \"author\": \"Lalatendu Swain\",\n    36→    \"source\": \"Medium\",\n    37→    \"url\": \"https://lalatenduswain.medium.com/top-10-ai-coding-cli-tools-in-2026-a-complete-developers-guide-3c63221bc83e\",\n    38→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-08\",\n    39→    \"topics\": [\"ai-coding\", \"cli-tools\", \"developer-tools\", \"tool-selection\"],\n    40→    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n    41→  },\n    42→  {\n    43→    \"title\": \"Revisiting Using AI Coding Assistants: You're Holding It Wrong Edition\",\n    44→    \"description\": \"Developer-focused discussion examining proper usage patterns for AI coding assistants and common mistakes developers make when integrating AI tools into their workflows. Highlights best practices and anti-patterns in AI-assisted development.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Hackaday\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"Tech Blog\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://hackaday.com/2026/06/08/revisiting-using-ai-coding-assistants-youre-holding-it-wrong-edition/\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-08\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"ai-coding\", \"best-practices\", \"workflow-integration\", \"developer-experience\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"AI Con USA 2026: Model Context Protocol and Prompt Engineering Workshops\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"Coverage from AI Con USA featuring workshops on Model Context Protocol Fundamentals and Prompt Engineering for Software Practitioners. Industry event focused on practical AI development skills and emerging standards.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"AI Con USA\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Industry Conference\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://aiconusa.com/\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-08\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"ai-development\", \"industry-standards\", \"education\", \"prompt-engineering\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"OpenAI Launches Lockdown Mode to Prevent Data Exfiltration\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"OpenAI rolled out Lockdown Mode for eligible accounts to help prevent the final stage of data exfiltration from prompt injection attacks by limiting outbound network requests. The feature directly attacks the 'exfiltration vectors' leg of the Lethal Trifecta (private data access + untrusted content exposure + data theft transmission). Simon Willison notes this implies ChatGPT's default settings don't provide robust protection against determined data exfiltration attacks.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-06-05\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"prompt-injection\", \"llms\", \"openai\", \"lethal-trifecta\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    71→  }\n    72→]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system